## Summary

`server_prompt_cache`, the RAM-backed cross-slot prompt/KV cache behind `--cache-ram` (default: enabled, 8192 MiB) and `--cache-idle-slots` (default: enabled), can restore content from a **completely unrelated, already-finished conversation** into a slot serving a **new, unrelated request**, and the model then generates a continuation of that stale conversation instead of answering the actual prompt. This reproduces with `--kv-unified` explicitly off (`-np 2`, no `-kvu`), so it is a distinct mechanism from #25992.

The API's own `usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` accounting shows `0` in every corrupted response observed, there is no client-visible signal that this happened.

## Root cause

`--cache-ram` (default 8192 MiB) and `--cache-idle-slots` (default `true`) are both **enabled by default** and not opt-in:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/common/common.h#L612-L615

```cpp
bool    cache_idle_slots    = true;  // save and clear idle slots upon starting a new task
...
int32_t cache_ram_mib       = 8192;  // -1 = no limit, 0 - disable, 1 = 1 MiB, etc.
```

With these on, `cache_idle_slots` publishes a snapshot of any idle slot's current prompt into the shared, cross-slot `server_prompt_cache` pool on **every task launch** (not just at eviction):

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-context.cpp#L2352-L2367

```cpp
if (params_base.cache_idle_slots) {
    for (auto & slot : slots) {
        if (!slot.is_processing()) {
            ...
            if (slot.prompt_save(*prompt_cache)) {
                ...
                prompt_cache->update();
            }
            if (params_base.kv_unified) {
                // [TAG_IDLE_SLOT_CLEAR]
                slot.prompt_clear();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Note the idle slot's own live VRAM KV/`prompt.tokens` is **not** cleared when `kv_unified` is false, only a RAM copy is published. So with `-np 2` (our config; `kv_unified` defaults false since `-np` is never `auto`), the pool is refreshed on nearly every request, and each slot keeps its own stale prompt history live until something overwrites it.

When `get_available_slot()` can't find a live slot with ≥`slot_prompt_similarity` (default 0.1) LCP against a new task, it falls back to LRU-evicting a slot, then tries the pool:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-context.cpp#L1577-L1594

```cpp
if (ret) {
    update_cache = update_cache && prompt_cache;
    update_cache = update_cache && task.type == SERVER_TASK_TYPE_COMPLETION;

    if (update_cache) {
        ret->prompt_save(*prompt_cache);

        if (!ret->prompt_load(*prompt_cache, task.tokens)) {
            ret->prompt_clear();
        }
        ...
```

`prompt_load()`'s return value gates `prompt_clear()`, but the return value doesn't mean what the caller assumes:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-task.cpp#L1790-L1815

```cpp
bool server_prompt_cache::load(server_prompt & prompt, const server_tokens & tokens_new, llama_context * ctx_tgt, llama_context * ctx_dft, int32_t id_slot) {
    ...
    auto it_best = states.end();

    for (auto it = states.begin(); it != states.end(); ++it) {
        const int lcp_cur = it->prompt.tokens.get_common_prefix(tokens_new);
        const float f_keep_cur = float(lcp_cur) / it->prompt.tokens.size();
        const float f_sim_cur  = float(lcp_cur) / tokens_new.size();

        // don't trash large prompts
        if (f_keep_cur < 0.25f) {
            continue;
        }
        ...
    }

    if (it_best != states.end()) {
        // restore full state via llama_state_seq_set_data_ext(), reassign prompt = it_best->prompt
    }

    return true; // <-- returns true (no-op) when it_best == states.end(), i.e. no match found
}
```

`load()` returns `true` even when `it_best == states.end()` (no match found), so `prompt_clear()` **never fires** in the normal "nothing in the pool is similar enough" case. The slot's `prompt.tokens` and live VRAM KV are left exactly as they were pre-eviction: a full, unrelated, already-finished conversation. Only `prompt_save()` ran, non-destructively snapshotting a copy elsewhere.

The main decode path later recomputes a real LCP against the actual new prompt and truncates (`keep_first(n_past)` + `mem.seq_rm(slot.id, p0, -1)`) before decoding, this is presumably intended to fully purge the stale content regardless of the above. Empirically, under concurrent two-slot load, it does not reliably do so (see repro below); we have not fully traced why that truncation is insufficient, only that it demonstrably is.

## Repro

Hardware: 2x AMD Strix Halo (gfx1151, ROCm/HIP), `llama-server` built at merge commit `a0e27a3e4` (`origin/master` = `adb55e514` above, merged onto our fork with two local, non-`tools/server` patches, confirmed byte-identical to upstream for every file quoted here).

```
--model <Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q8_0.gguf> --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080 --n-gpu-layers 99 \
--ctx-size 393216 --parallel 2 --cache-type-k q8_0 --cache-type-v q8_0 --flash-attn on \
--threads 16 --ubatch-size 2048 --direct-io --jinja \
--chat-template-file Qwen3.5-4B.jinja --metrics --timeout 240
```

(No `--cache-ram`, `--cache-idle-slots`, or `--kv-unified` passed, all three defaults apply. `-np 2` means `kv_unified` is genuinely `false` per `server.cpp`'s `n_parallel < 0` check, ruling out #25992's mechanism directly.)

Script (pseudocode, full version available on request):
1. Send several sequential `/v1/chat/completions` requests, each with a unique, randomly-generated ~1200-word system prompt + a user message + an assistant `tool_calls` turn + a `tool` result turn (~4500 tokens total), each containing a unique marker string and instructing the model to echo a derived "secret code" back. This seeds the RAM pool wit

### Environment
- llama.cpp b10375 (ba360efe1), official ROCm 7.14 prebuilt
- ROCm 7.14.60850, gfx1151 (Strix Halo, AMD AI MAX 395)
- Model: DeepSeek-V4-Flash GGUF (UD-Q8_K_XL, 5 shards)
- Dual-node setup: llama-server + ggml-rpc-server

### Symptoms
Loading DeepSeek V4 with `--ctx-size > 131072` works, but any prefill crashes immediately. With `--ctx-size 131072` everything runs fine.

Production crash (RPC host, HIP backend):

```
ggml_cuda_compute_forward: TOP_K failed
ROCm error: invalid configuration argument
  current device: 0, in function ggml_cuda_compute_forward
  at ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu:2374
-> ggml_abort / SIGABRT
```

### Minimal repro (single machine, no model, no RPC)

The program below calls `ggml_top_k` + `ggml_backend_graph_compute` directly on a HIP backend (no supports_op check, same as the RPC server code path):

```cpp
// topk_repro.cpp — build: hipcc -O2 topk_repro.cpp -I<src>/ggml/include
//   -I<src>/ggml/src -I<src>/include -L<build>/bin -lggml -lggml-base
//   -lggml-cpu -lggml-hip -Wl,-rpath,<build>/bin
#include "ggml.h"
#include "ggml-backend.h"
#include "ggml-cuda.h"
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <vector>

int main(int argc, char ** argv) {
    int64_t ncols = 8192;  // DS4 @1M ctx: 1048576/128 = 8192 lid blocks
    int64_t nrows = 8;
    int     k     = 512;   // deepseek4.attention.indexer.top_k
    if (argc >= 4) { ncols = atoll(argv[1]); nrows = atoll(argv[2]); k = atoi(argv[3]); }

    ggml_backend_t backend = ggml_backend_cuda_init(0);
    if (!backend) { fprintf(stderr, "no HIP backend\n"); return 1; }

    ggml_init_params params = { 64u << 20, nullptr, true };
    ggml_context * ctx = ggml_init(params);
    ggml_tensor * src  = ggml_new_tensor_2d(ctx, GGML_TYPE_F32, ncols, nrows);
    ggml_tensor * topk = ggml_top_k(ctx, src, k);
    ggml_cgraph * graph = ggml_new_graph(ctx);
    ggml_build_forward_expand(graph, topk);

    ggml_gallocr * allocr = ggml_gallocr_new(ggml_backend_get_default_buffer_type(backend));
    if (!ggml_gallocr_reserve(allocr, graph)) { fprintf(stderr, "gallocr reserve failed\n"); return 1; }
    ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft(ctx, ggml_backend_get_default_buffer_type(backend));
    ggml_gallocr_alloc_graph(allocr, graph);

    std::vector<float> host(ggml_nelements(src));
    for (auto & v : host) { v = (float) rand() / RAND_MAX; }
    ggml_backend_tensor_set(src, host.data(), 0, ggml_nbytes(src));

    fprintf(stderr, "computing top_k: ncols=%lld nrows=%lld k=%d\n",
            (long long) ncols, (long long) nrows, k);
    ggml_backend_graph_compute(backend, graph);
    fprintf(stderr, "compute OK\n");
    ggml_gallocr_free(allocr);
    ggml_free(ctx);
    ggml_backend_free(backend);
    return 0;
}
```

```
./topk_repro 1024 8 512   # -> compute OK
./topk_repro 1025 8 512   # -> TOP_K failed / invalid configuration argument
```

The boundary is exactly 1024.

### Root cause

- `GGML_CUDA_USE_CUB` is never defined for HIP builds (`ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh:111` requires `!GGML_USE_HIP && !GGML_USE_MUSA && CUDART_VERSION >= 11070`). So on ROCm, `GGML_OP_TOP_K` always takes the pure-bitonic path (top-k.cu `#else` branch).
- `argsort_f32_i32_cuda_bitonic` launches with `block_dims = (next_power_of_2(ncols), 1, 1)`. GPU block size is capped at 1024 threads, so any `ncols > 1024` launches an invalid grid -> `hipErrorInvalidConfiguration`.
- `ggml_cuda_supports_op()` correctly declares TOP_K unsupported for `ne[0] > 1024` when CUB is absent (`ggml-cuda.cu:5124-5127`), but that check is bypassed in practice: the RPC server executes the pre-scheduled graph without re-checking support, so the invalid kernel launch reaches the HIP backend.

### Why this blocks DeepSeek V4 ctx > 128K

DeepSeek V4's lightning indexer calls `ggml_top_k` per layer (`src/models/deepseek4.cpp:699`) with `indexer_score->ne[0] = ctx / attention.sliding_window`. For this model `sliding_window == 128`, so:

- ctx 131072 -> 1024 blocks -> bitonic path (works)
- ctx 1048576 -> 8192 blocks -> crash

### Suggested fix directions

1. Make the bitonic argsort handle `ncols > 1024` (multi-block grid per row), or
2. On HIP builds, fall back to rocPRIM segmented sort for `ncols > 1024` instead of bitonic, or
3. Have the RPC backend forward `supports_op` from the remote device so the scheduler falls back to CPU for unsupported shapes.

